4.  CHANGES FROM PREVIOUS REVISIONS

4.1 CHANGES OF RELEASE PACKAGE REV. E FROM PACKAGE REV. D1

4.1.1 ASM800 Rev. E

The following changes were made to ASM800 Rev. D1:
1.  Overlapping code  problem.
    If the user sets the location counter to a value less than
    its current value then the assembler issues a warning of a  possi-
    ble  overlap in code. 
2.  Underscore acceptance in .title directive.
    The assembler now accepts underscore as a valid character in  the
    .title directive. 
3.  Error code return.
    ASM800 now generates a return code of 0 when no errors are  found
    during  assembly,  and 1 if errors are encountered.  Warnings are
    not considered errors.
4.  Forced listing with  /FL  option.
    This new switch overrides  all  listing  directives  and forces a
    listing on Pass Two.
5.  Symbol file generation.
    The  command  line  switch  /S  now causes a separate symbol file
    to be generated.  The file will have an extension of .SYM and 
    contains the same information as in the symbol listing in the .PRN
    file generated by ASM800.

4.2 CHANGES OF RELEASE PACKAGE REV. D1 FROM PACKAGE REV.D

4.2.1 ASM800 Rev. D1

The following changes were made to ASM800 Rev. D:
1. A warning  is  now  generated for JP .+1 (a one byte jump to following
   location) to remind the user that a NOP opcode is generated
   (there is no  JP  .+1 opcode).  A NOP is a different number of
   instruction cycles than a JP opcode.
2. Division by zero will now generate  error  message;
   previously  assembly aborted on this error.
3. When an extension is specified on an object file, e.g. /O=z.qq,
   the extension is no longer forced to .LM. (If no extension or filename is 
   specified, the default extension is still .LM.)

4.3 CHANGES OF PACKAGE REV. D FROM PACKAGE REV. C

4.3.1 ASM800 Rev. D

The following changes were made to ASM800 Rev. B:
1. Instructions for  COP888 are now supported.  See .CHIP below.
2. The .CHIP directive is required before any code. (If the 
   appropriate COP8xx.INC file  is included at start of source,
   this is taken care of; see installation instructions.)
   The valid .CHIP arguments  are  820,840,888; these select proper
   instruction set and proper ROM size (the ROM size check can be
   overridden;  see .MAXROM directive below.)
3. The .MAXROM  directive  is  added; it is used to override the default
   ROM size selected by the .CHIP directive. The argument of .MAXROM
   is  the address of the last valid ROM byte. If the .MAXROM direc-
   tive is not used, the following are the default  values  provided
   by the .CHIP directive:
   03FF Hex for .CHIP 820       (1K ROM)
   07FF Hex for .CHIP 840       (2K ROM)
   0FFF Hex for .CHIP 888       (4K ROM)

   The argument of .MAXROM must be less than 07FFF Hex, and  .MAXROM
   must  appear  before any code. 
4. The .ADDRW directive is added; it is identical to the .WORD 
   directive, except that the  byte  order in the word are reversed,
   i.e. high byte, then low byte. This is the correct order for the 
   VIS address tables.

The following bugs were fixed in ASM800 Rev. B:
1. .MDEL directive now properly deletes macros with .MOLC symbols.
2. In macro, ^symbol where symbol is >9999, now correctly shows
   five  decimal  digits. 
3. In macro,  an  error is now given if a ^symbol or @symbol is forward
   referenced. This avoids phase errors in some cases.
4. A .TITLE  or .FORM string of 26 characters now is correctly
   shown in header.
5. The symbol and macro table listing now has page breaks,  even  if
   .LIST has disabled listing. 
6. LD address,#immediate, where address or immediate is forward
   referenced, no longer generates  a  phase error on following labels.
7. The .INCLD directive now generates an error when no filename is given.
8. Filenames may  now  start  with numeric characters.


4.3.2 LMHEX Rev. D

The following bug is fixed in LMHEX Rev. C:

A carriage return-line feed is now generated after  the  hex  end
record.

4.3.3 HEXLM Rev.D

The following bug is fixed in HEXLM Rev. C:

Hex records with up to 255 bytes of data are now accepted (previ-
ous maximum was 16 bytes of data).

4.4  CHANGES OF PACKAGE REV. C FROM PACKAGE REV. B 

4.4.1 ASM800 Rev. B

The following bugs were fixed in ASM800 Rev. A:

1. The ASCII character ~ now  correctly  passes  from  the  assembly
source to the listing.
2. the L() and H() operators can now operate on a forward  reference
expression without generating an addressing mode error.


4.5  CHANGES OF PACKAGE REV. B FROM PACKAGES REV. A

4.5.1  LMHEX Rev. C

The following bug is fixed in LMHEX Rev. B:

The correct record type (01) is now generated  for  the  Hex  end
record instead of record type (00).

4.5.2  HEXLM Rev. C

The following bug is fixed in HEXLM Rev. B:

The Hex end record type is now read for checksum calculation.




5.  COMPATIBILITY

5.1  COMPATIBILITY WITH PREVIOUS REVISIONS
Package rev. E is compatible with previous release packages,  ex-
cept for minor changes to ASM800 as noted in section 4.  File 
COP880.INC is added to support COP880 chip (see installation section 6).

5.2  COMPATIBILITY WITH RELATED PRODUCTS

5.2.1  MOLE
This software is compatible with the following MOLE firmware:

MOLE Brain Board COMM, Rev.B
MOLE Brain Board EXEC, Rev.B
MOLE COP820/840/880 Personality Board MONITOR, Rev. 4.00
MOLE COP888 Personality Board MONITOR, Rev. 2.00                
MOLE COP8620/COP8640 Personality Board MONITOR, Rev. 1.00                


6.  INSTALLATION PROCEDURE
Verify that the floppy disk (440410527-001) is correct by run-
ning the following procedure:
1. Put MOLE disk into a drive and logon that drive,  e.g.  if  drive
B,  type  B:
2. Type CD \ to get root directory
3. Type XCRC *.* to get sum of CRC's of files
4. Type TYPE XCRC\OUT and verify that  sum  of CRC's is same.
   If there is a discrepancy, or the disk cannot be read,
   contact NSC (See Sec 9 below).
The CONFIG.SYS file in your root directory must contain the line
FILES=16 (or greater); hard disk performance may be improved if
the line  BUFFERS = value is modified (see DOS Manual for best value).
Copy the .EXE and .COM files to a desired directory  (\COP800  is
recommended).    Modify   the   PATH  command  (normally  in  the
autoexec.bat file in your root directory) to include the  \COP800
(or another) directory, e.g.

 PATH=c:\             would be changed to
 PATH=c:\;c:\COP800   (if .EXE and .COM files in directory c:\COP800)

6.1 ASM800
The first line of all assembly source programs should contain the line:
  .INCLD COPxxx.INC
where xxx = 820,840,880 or 888 as appropriate.  Note that the include
file for COP8620 is COP820.INC, and the include file for COP8640 is
COP840.INC.
This include file contains the proper  .CHIP  directive,  defines
symbolic names for frequently used memory locations, and initial-
izes stack pointer to address location 02F Hex (for COP820 and COP8620) or ad-
dress  location  06F Hex for COP840, COP8640, and COP880). The user should
modify stack initialization address if necessary.
Note : All assembly source and include files must  be  in  the
current directory, as ASM800 does NOT accept pathnames.
Note: If the user chooses not to use the COPXXX.INC include file, he must 
include the following .CHIP/.MAXROM directives before the start of his code.
1)  For COP820, COP8620
    .CHIP 820

2)  For COP840, COP8640
    .CHIP 840

3)  For COP880
    .CHIP 840		; 820/840 instruction set
    .MAXROM 0FFF        ; forces ROM size of 4k

4)  For COP888
    .CHIP 888

Except for COP888, the stack pointer must be initialized also.

6.2 PC-TALK
This  public domain program is provided  for  terminal  emulation
and  general  communication usage.  This program is provided free
of charge and is not supported by NSC.  For  manual  and  support
refer to invocation screen of program.

6.3 COMM
COMM is ready to run using the COM1 serial port. If you  want  to
use  the COM2 port, rename COMM.COM to COMM1.COM (for backup) and
rename COMM2.COM to COMM.COM. 

You will need the following equipment to complete the installation
and verification:

1.  MOLE Brain Board (Product Name: MOLE-BRAIN)
2.  One MOLE  Personality Board.  Foe example, the MOLE COP820/840
PERSONALITY BOARD (Product Name: MOLE-COP8-PB1)
3.  5V DC Power supply, required amps
4.  Optional RS232 compatible CRT terminal.

To determine which type of RS232 cable to use to connect MOLE  to
IBM  PC,  first  read APPENDIX A of "PC-DOS COMMUNICATION PROGRAM
(COMM) SOFTWARE USER'S MANUAL". Typically, the  IBM  RS232  cable
supplied  with  your PC is DTE type, and will connect directly to
the J3 port of the MOLE (J1 port may also be used if jumper W2 on
MOLE  Brain Board is set to position 1-3.) If the IBM RS232 cable
is DCE type, connect to J2 port instead. 
No further  connections are necessary if you plan to use COMM's
converse mode on the PC as a terminal emulator for the MOLE;
skip the rest of  this  section.
If  you  plan to use a CRT connected to the MOLE, connect
the CRT to J1 using the cable supplied with the MOLE.  On  reset,
the  MOLE  will  default  to using J2 as the COMM port to the PC;
therefore, if the IBM RS232 cable (DTE type) is connected to  J3,
do ONE of the following:

      After every MOLE power-on (or reset), enter the following
      command to set COMM port to J3:
      X>STATUS SET SD=S3
      OR construct the inverting RS232 cable shown in APPENDIX A,
      Figure A-2, of "PCDOS COMMUNICATION PROGRAM (COMM) SOFTWARE
      USER'S MANUAL".  The cable connects between the  IBM  cable
  and
      J2.
Remember that on power-on or reset, the MOLE sets J1,J2,  and  J3
to  the  RS232 parameters of the CRT on J1.  If necessary, adjust
the RS232 parameters of J2 or J3 using the MOLE  STATUS  command,
before using COMM.

The installation procedure is:

 COMM is already installed for COM1 port (or  COM2  port  if  you
 modified  COMM  above).   Use the MS-DOS MODE program to set the
 port parameters; enter:
  MODE COMx:baud,N,8,1
 where COMx is COM1 or COM2, and baud is baud rate.   The  recom-
 mended baud rate is 9600, with the following restrictions:
 File transfer has no restriction on baud rate.  However, in con-
 verse  mode,  console output characters may be lost if baud rate
 is greater than 300 baud (the exact baud rate depends on the  PC
 hardware).  It is recommended to limit the baud rate if converse
 mode is used; or optionally, the baud rate can be set to 9600 if
 the  following procedure is followed (enter italicized commands,
 assume disk with MODE and COMM program in drive A):

  A>MODE COMx:9600,N,8,1       ;COMx = COM1 or COM2
  A>COMM                       ;call up COMM program
  #>C                          ;enter converse mode

  Now reset MOLE!!!!

  <carriage return>            ;enter carriage return key
  -XR:5E8X>                    ;garbled sign-on line from MOLE
                               ;(exact response may vary)
  ->STATUS Sx CH=10,CR=10,LF=50;Sx is S1,S2,S3 depending on connection
                               ;to J1,J2,J3 of MOLE respectively.
                               ;(see MOLE BRAIN BOARD USER'S MANUAL
                               ; for explanation of command; delay
                               ; values required may be less depending
                               ; on speed of PC)
                               ;
  -X>                          ;MOLE should now respond
                               ;without characters being lost
                               ;This procedure will not slow down
                               ;COMM download
 Optionally, the PC-TALK program can be used for terminal  emula-
 tion with the MOLE, with COMM used only for download.
 To verify that the COMM download is functional, follow  the  se-
 quence of commands described in the COMM manual, Appendix C.



7.  KNOWN SOFTWARE PRODUCTS ERRORS AND MANUAL UPDATES

 Manual Updates:

 See file ASMUPDAT.MAN on disk.


 Software errors:
1.  ASM800 does NOT accept  filenames  which  include
    pathnames,  or  filenames which contain non-alphanumeric characters.
2.  During macro expansion, the error message "EXPRESSION NOT
 DEFINED  DURING  PASS 1" incorrectly occurs, for a concatenation
 operation.


